WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:05.600
Iain: There are an enormous number of aspects
of modernist culture, the culture of the last

00:00:05.600 --> 00:00:12.100
hundred years or so, which simulates schizophrenia.

00:00:12.100 --> 00:00:24.010
Brent: Well, it's my pleasure to welcome Dr.
Iain McGilchrist, psychiatrist and author

00:00:24.010 --> 00:00:28.539
of "The Master and His Emissary: The Divided
Brain and the Making of the Western World"

00:00:28.539 --> 00:00:30.490
here to AEI.

00:00:30.490 --> 00:00:35.800
Dr. McGilchrist holds multiple fellowships
at Oxford University, the Royal College of

00:00:35.800 --> 00:00:38.409
Psychiatrists, and the Royal Society of Arts.

00:00:38.409 --> 00:00:39.409
So, welcome, Iain.

00:00:39.409 --> 00:00:40.409
It's good to have you.

00:00:40.409 --> 00:00:41.670
Iain: Thank you very much, Brent.

00:00:41.670 --> 00:00:47.249
Brent: So, I hear from others that one question
authors don't usually get asked when they

00:00:47.249 --> 00:00:50.639
come on to a program like this is, what's
your book about?

00:00:50.639 --> 00:00:55.579
So, why don't you start there and give us
a little bit of what your book is about?

00:00:55.579 --> 00:01:01.399
Iain: Well, it's about the vexed topic
of the difference between the brain hemispheres,

00:01:01.399 --> 00:01:08.950
a topic that has become rather toxic topic
because of its exploitation by media people

00:01:08.950 --> 00:01:13.000
and pop psychology.

00:01:13.000 --> 00:01:19.790
And it started with things that we learnt...well,
it took off, let me say, because we learned

00:01:19.790 --> 00:01:25.590
differences about the brain hemispheres in
the 19th century, but it really took off after

00:01:25.590 --> 00:01:33.110
the first split-brain operations in Caltech
in the 1960s where the brain was divided,

00:01:33.110 --> 00:01:39.539
this was for treatment of intractable epilepsy,
and it gave people an insight into what the

00:01:39.539 --> 00:01:42.140
two halves of the brain might do that were
different.

00:01:42.140 --> 00:01:48.259
And, out of that came a number of ideas that
eventually were proved to be wrong.

00:01:48.259 --> 00:01:52.740
They were, for example, that the left hemisphere
does language and reason and the right hemisphere

00:01:52.740 --> 00:01:55.439
does emotion and pictures.

00:01:55.439 --> 00:01:59.280
And, with further research, it was found that,
in fact, both hemispheres were involved with

00:01:59.280 --> 00:02:07.359
everything, but that was the bit that we didn't
notice, was that they were involved consistently

00:02:07.359 --> 00:02:10.150
in two entirely different ways.

00:02:10.150 --> 00:02:15.530
And, the subject had fallen into disrepute
because we were only asking the machine question,

00:02:15.530 --> 00:02:17.210
what does it do?

00:02:17.210 --> 00:02:20.140
And finding that they're both involved in
doing everything.

00:02:20.140 --> 00:02:24.550
But, the question...the more human question,
and, after all, the brain is part of a person,

00:02:24.550 --> 00:02:26.760
"In what way do they do?"

00:02:26.760 --> 00:02:32.190
Was not being asked, but if you ask that question,
you get a fascinating series of answers.

00:02:32.190 --> 00:02:39.780
And, my book is really expounding what those
differences are between the two brain hemispheres,

00:02:39.780 --> 00:02:45.680
and what inference that might have on the
history of culture, including, and specifically,

00:02:45.680 --> 00:02:47.530
our own culture now.

00:02:47.530 --> 00:02:53.230
Brent: You talk about the right hemisphere
of the brain as being the master...

00:02:53.230 --> 00:02:54.230
Iain: Yes.

00:02:54.230 --> 00:02:57.400
Brent: ...and you talk about the left hemisphere
as being the emissary.

00:02:57.400 --> 00:02:58.400
Iain: Yes.

00:02:58.400 --> 00:03:01.010
Brent: Tell us what you mean by that.

00:03:01.010 --> 00:03:09.060
Iain: Well, the image comes from a story of
a wise spiritual master who looked after a

00:03:09.060 --> 00:03:13.420
community so well that it flourished and grew,
and after a while he discovered that he not

00:03:13.420 --> 00:03:20.780
only couldn't look after everything that was
needed by these people, this community, but

00:03:20.780 --> 00:03:25.980
that importantly he couldn't be involved in
certain things if he was to maintain his overview.

00:03:25.980 --> 00:03:32.380
So, he delegated that job of going about and
doing certain quite specific detailed, administrative

00:03:32.380 --> 00:03:37.970
tasks to his brightest and best servant.

00:03:37.970 --> 00:03:42.570
The trouble with this guy was that he was
bright, but not bright enough to know what

00:03:42.570 --> 00:03:45.730
it was he didn't know, and so he thought,
"I know everything.

00:03:45.730 --> 00:03:46.770
What does the master know?

00:03:46.770 --> 00:03:47.770
He doesn't know anything.

00:03:47.770 --> 00:03:51.750
He's sitting back there smiling seraphically
back at base.

00:03:51.750 --> 00:03:52.780
He doesn't know anything.

00:03:52.780 --> 00:03:54.760
I'm the one that does all the heavy lifting.

00:03:54.760 --> 00:03:56.170
I really understand."

00:03:56.170 --> 00:03:58.590
The trouble is he didn't know what it was
he didn't know.

00:03:58.590 --> 00:04:00.510
He didn't know what it was the master knew.

00:04:00.510 --> 00:04:01.630
The master knew he needed the emissary.

00:04:01.630 --> 00:04:04.680
The emissary didn't know he needed the master.

00:04:04.680 --> 00:04:10.050
And this led to the collapse of the community,
and this is a parable.

00:04:10.050 --> 00:04:17.220
The parable, I think, applies to the relationship
between the two neuronal systems, the two

00:04:17.220 --> 00:04:18.959
hemispheres.

00:04:18.959 --> 00:04:24.790
One has a broad view, which includes the need
for a detailed view.

00:04:24.790 --> 00:04:31.820
It has a global view that sees the need for
certain quite specific facts and data.

00:04:31.820 --> 00:04:39.460
The other hemisphere, and this is the left
hemisphere, sees only the details, but has

00:04:39.460 --> 00:04:42.060
no concept that it's missing the big picture.

00:04:42.060 --> 00:04:45.920
It thinks that maybe you can put all these
details together and they'll compose the big

00:04:45.920 --> 00:04:48.320
picture, but actually it's not like that.

00:04:48.320 --> 00:04:54.230
We think, and the left hemisphere can't see
that it could be otherwise, that a whole is

00:04:54.230 --> 00:04:57.000
simply made by putting together the parts.

00:04:57.000 --> 00:05:01.940
But, actually, the whole of many things, as
we appreciate in living, is not the same as

00:05:01.940 --> 00:05:04.670
what you get when you take it apart into a
handful of pieces.

00:05:04.670 --> 00:05:10.290
Brent: And so, you've used some interesting
examples in the past about this from the animal

00:05:10.290 --> 00:05:11.290
kingdom...

00:05:11.290 --> 00:05:12.290
Iain: Yes.

00:05:12.290 --> 00:05:16.380
Brent: ...and how you sort of...how the penny
dropped as it were when you started studying

00:05:16.380 --> 00:05:20.030
animal behavior.

00:05:20.030 --> 00:05:25.470
Could you talk a little bit about that, because
that helps to explain this left-right hemisphere

00:05:25.470 --> 00:05:26.470
distinction?

00:05:26.470 --> 00:05:27.470
Iain: Yes.

00:05:27.470 --> 00:05:31.620
Well, it's not just human beings that have
lateralized brains, in other words, that have

00:05:31.620 --> 00:05:34.500
two halves of the brain that are not symmetrical.

00:05:34.500 --> 00:05:39.000
By the way, they're not symmetrical in anything
you can measure, anatomically or physiologically.

00:05:39.000 --> 00:05:43.260
So, functionally, structurally, they are different,
reliably so.

00:05:43.260 --> 00:05:45.420
But this is not just about humans.

00:05:45.420 --> 00:05:46.760
This is not even about apes.

00:05:46.760 --> 00:05:53.540
It's not even about mammals, or reptiles,
or amphibians, or fish, or insects, or nematode

00:05:53.540 --> 00:05:54.540
worms.

00:05:54.540 --> 00:06:00.720
Every existing living thing that we know that
has any kind of neuronal structure at all

00:06:00.720 --> 00:06:06.320
has an asymmetrical one, and that leads to
the question, "Why?"

00:06:06.320 --> 00:06:14.030
And there is an answer to this, which gradually
dawned on me, which is that it has an important

00:06:14.030 --> 00:06:21.220
evolutionary significance, that is that it
solves a conundrum, how to eat and stay alive,

00:06:21.220 --> 00:06:26.630
and that doesn't sound like a conundrum to
us now, but for most animals, it's a dangerous

00:06:26.630 --> 00:06:32.500
business being focused on one thing because
you're obviously not looking at everything

00:06:32.500 --> 00:06:33.500
else.

00:06:33.500 --> 00:06:38.850
So, while you're focusing on catching that
rabbit, picking out that seed against the

00:06:38.850 --> 00:06:42.800
background of grit, you know, picking up a
twig to build the nest, you've got to have

00:06:42.800 --> 00:06:47.510
very sharply focused attention, just very
narrow view attention to a detail.

00:06:47.510 --> 00:06:53.700
But if that's the only attention you're paying,
you'll soon become somebody else's lunch while

00:06:53.700 --> 00:07:01.210
you're getting your own, because you need
to have a sustained vigilance for predators,

00:07:01.210 --> 00:07:05.110
for conspecifics, for others that are around,
and for everything else basically that is

00:07:05.110 --> 00:07:07.110
going on in the world.

00:07:07.110 --> 00:07:12.310
And, when you observe animals and bird, what
you find is that the left hemisphere serves

00:07:12.310 --> 00:07:18.240
the predator instinct in which you go for
some detail, and therefore is good for manipulating

00:07:18.240 --> 00:07:19.240
the world.

00:07:19.240 --> 00:07:22.210
But it's not terribly clever, it doesn't really
see the whole picture, it doesn't understand

00:07:22.210 --> 00:07:26.889
what it's doing, but it's a servant, it's
a very good servant.

00:07:26.889 --> 00:07:31.840
The right hemisphere, meanwhile, is understanding
the whole thing and saying, "I see where this

00:07:31.840 --> 00:07:36.360
thing of getting my lunch and getting that
twig for the...how that fits into the whole."

00:07:36.360 --> 00:07:42.820
And, what I am suggesting is that in the way
in which we now think, we've got locked on

00:07:42.820 --> 00:07:48.680
to a rather unintelligent way of thinking
that enables you to be very efficient at grasping,

00:07:48.680 --> 00:07:50.620
but not very efficient at understanding.

00:07:50.620 --> 00:07:54.270
And you think about it, the left hemisphere
is the part of your brain that controls your

00:07:54.270 --> 00:07:58.680
right hand, which is the one with which you
do all the grasping, and it's the one that

00:07:58.680 --> 00:08:03.990
controls the bits of language, not all of
language, but the bits that enable you to

00:08:03.990 --> 00:08:05.570
say, "Oh, I've grasped it."

00:08:05.570 --> 00:08:12.020
In other words, you've got some little certain
factoid, but the problem is you try to live

00:08:12.020 --> 00:08:16.580
a wise life on the basis of putting a lot
of these together, and you simply can't.

00:08:16.580 --> 00:08:22.840
Brent: You've covered pretty well for us the
scientific side of this, but that's only half

00:08:22.840 --> 00:08:24.530
of your book.

00:08:24.530 --> 00:08:32.079
The second half of your book is really devoted
to an analysis, a cultural analysis, historical

00:08:32.079 --> 00:08:38.229
analysis, and I'd like you to unpack that
a little bit, you know, in terms of what you

00:08:38.229 --> 00:08:45.480
think your research tells us about the development
of the West, Western civilization.

00:08:45.480 --> 00:08:51.639
Iain: The trigger for this came from a book
by a distinguished colleague called Louis

00:08:51.639 --> 00:08:56.019
Sass who wrote a book called "Madness and
Modernism," and effectively what that book

00:08:56.019 --> 00:09:02.529
demonstrated was that there are an enormous
number of aspects of modernist culture, the

00:09:02.529 --> 00:09:09.050
culture of the last hundred years or so, which
simulates schizophrenia.

00:09:09.050 --> 00:09:14.980
In other words, people who suffer from schizophrenia
describe experiences, depict phenomena that

00:09:14.980 --> 00:09:18.930
are extraordinary like those that are in the
mainstream of modernism.

00:09:18.930 --> 00:09:26.620
Now, that was a...and, over 500 pages, he
shows this time and time again in great detail.

00:09:26.620 --> 00:09:28.060
It's very convincing.

00:09:28.060 --> 00:09:35.399
For example, many of the movements in the
visual arts in the last hundred years exemplify

00:09:35.399 --> 00:09:41.029
various phenomena of distorted vision which
are found in people with right hemisphere

00:09:41.029 --> 00:09:42.029
damage.

00:09:42.029 --> 00:09:48.759
So, some of the actual movements that became
popular exemplified phenomena that until now

00:09:48.759 --> 00:09:53.360
have been quite peculiar to people with schizophrenia.

00:09:53.360 --> 00:10:02.559
And, you see it also in, for example, the
literature where a deliberate effect of sense

00:10:02.559 --> 00:10:08.101
of alienation that perhaps other people are
machines are not really living, but they're

00:10:08.101 --> 00:10:14.529
almost like sort of terrifying zombies, or
the sense that you yourself are cut off and

00:10:14.529 --> 00:10:20.970
unable to feel for anything, and the distortion
of perspective, the deliberate disruption

00:10:20.970 --> 00:10:25.970
of narrative which has been part of the way
in which novels have been written at periods

00:10:25.970 --> 00:10:33.019
during the last hundred years, and the willingness
to strip things away from context and deliberately

00:10:33.019 --> 00:10:42.459
make them seem strange or odd creates an effect
which is awfully like the experiences that

00:10:42.459 --> 00:10:45.449
people with schizophrenia describe.

00:10:45.449 --> 00:10:52.839
They seem to be experiencing something that
modernist artists, writers, and even philosophers

00:10:52.839 --> 00:10:58.949
seem to be tackling, which is this kind of
devitalization, this alienation, this disembodiment,

00:10:58.949 --> 00:11:04.560
this mechanization, and so forth, which comes
out in the art and culture.

00:11:04.560 --> 00:11:07.660
So, question is, "Why?"

00:11:07.660 --> 00:11:13.670
And this struck me like a thunderbolt because
I've been researching at Johns Hopkins in

00:11:13.670 --> 00:11:21.260
Baltimore on asymmetries in the brain of people
with schizophrenia, and what I had learned,

00:11:21.260 --> 00:11:24.880
amongst many other things, apart from the
fact that their brains are not asymmetrical

00:11:24.880 --> 00:11:30.290
in the way that ours are, is that people with
schizophrenia are awfully like people with

00:11:30.290 --> 00:11:32.769
right-hemisphere brain damage.

00:11:32.769 --> 00:11:39.259
People with right-hemisphere strokes, or tumors,
or injuries experience things, describe things,

00:11:39.259 --> 00:11:42.809
depict things in the way that people with
schizophrenia do, broadly.

00:11:42.809 --> 00:11:48.410
I mean, there are differences, but that's
a very substantial similarity.

00:11:48.410 --> 00:11:53.459
And so, what I took from this was that it
wasn't that we were all becoming schizophrenic,

00:11:53.459 --> 00:11:59.149
but that maybe we were not utilizing what
our right hemispheres tell us.

00:11:59.149 --> 00:12:02.830
We were listening only to what our left hemispheres
tell us.

00:12:02.830 --> 00:12:07.519
And, I looked at that for quite a long time
and saw masses of evidence that this seemed

00:12:07.519 --> 00:12:13.420
to hold up, and I then thought, well, if that's
the case, and in the past we've had, you know,

00:12:13.420 --> 00:12:18.679
different kinds of culture, I mean, many movements
in the history of ideas in the West, they

00:12:18.679 --> 00:12:23.100
presumably weren't at all so skewed in this
way.

00:12:23.100 --> 00:12:31.040
And I looked at the ancient Greek civilization,
at the Roman civilization, and our own modern

00:12:31.040 --> 00:12:35.959
civilization, starting with the Renaissance
and moving forwards through big movements

00:12:35.959 --> 00:12:44.829
like the Reformation, the Enlightenment, romanticism,
modernism, and post-modernism, and to abbreviate

00:12:44.829 --> 00:12:51.779
a much longer story, effectively what I saw
there was the same shape repeated.

00:12:51.779 --> 00:12:58.399
And that shape goes like this, at the beginning
of a civilization, it seems suddenly to erupt

00:12:58.399 --> 00:13:08.250
with a great efflorescence of ideas, thoughts,
creative material in poetry, in drama, in

00:13:08.250 --> 00:13:16.920
history-making, in the writing of myths, in
the exploration of the seas, the stars, the

00:13:16.920 --> 00:13:23.779
use of mathematics, even the beginnings of
engineering, and these things then, over time,

00:13:23.779 --> 00:13:29.360
which are so effective and so flourishing,
quite soon, over a period of usually about

00:13:29.360 --> 00:13:34.059
400 or 500 years, deteriorate in a recognizable
way.

00:13:34.059 --> 00:13:40.069
The society becomes more rigid, it becomes
more steeply hierarchical, it becomes more

00:13:40.069 --> 00:13:46.889
bureaucratic, it becomes more interested in
control and power than it is in actually imagination,

00:13:46.889 --> 00:13:51.660
which requires that you make yourself vulnerable
in certain ways, and it simply can't afford

00:13:51.660 --> 00:13:52.990
to do this.

00:13:52.990 --> 00:13:58.009
And the Greeks overreached themselves with
their civilization, the Romans did the same,

00:13:58.009 --> 00:13:59.199
and they collapsed.

00:13:59.199 --> 00:14:03.779
Now, in our own case, I believe that we're
following the same path, that we were very

00:14:03.779 --> 00:14:09.009
rich in creativity for a few hundred years,
and that in the last...certainly, particularly

00:14:09.009 --> 00:14:20.360
the last 150 years, there has been an overemphasis
on domination, getting empire, first, notably

00:14:20.360 --> 00:14:26.379
the British and then more in a commercial
sense, the Americans, and cultural domination,

00:14:26.379 --> 00:14:32.639
in which I think a very diminished sort of
bureaucratic...necessarily so because when

00:14:32.639 --> 00:14:34.690
you've got an empire, you know, what are you
gonna do?

00:14:34.690 --> 00:14:37.352
You're going to be emphasizing the military,
and the bureaucracy, and the administration...

00:14:37.352 --> 00:14:39.860
Brent: It has to be managed.

00:14:39.860 --> 00:14:42.019
Iain: It has to be managed.

00:14:42.019 --> 00:14:51.019
And so, management think, which to me is the
death of imagination, becomes, if you like,

00:14:51.019 --> 00:14:53.269
the way in which everything is interpreted.

00:14:53.269 --> 00:15:00.019
It's interpreted as essentially knowable,
quantitative, objective, and essentially dead,

00:15:00.019 --> 00:15:05.579
whereas, in fact, everything we experience
is changing, animate, and multiply connected

00:15:05.579 --> 00:15:07.119
to everything else we know.

00:15:07.119 --> 00:15:16.100
So, one living animate, changing... pleasantly,
constantly leading one beyond the horizons

00:15:16.100 --> 00:15:23.279
of what one knows now, that kind of a world
gets eliminated in favor of a dead, static,

00:15:23.279 --> 00:15:24.990
contained, knowable nothing.

00:15:24.990 --> 00:15:27.839
That's where we're at.

00:15:27.839 --> 00:15:32.120
Brent: Well, that's a little daunting.

00:15:32.120 --> 00:15:39.470
So, tell me how you see...just to build on
that a bit, for the average person who's thinking

00:15:39.470 --> 00:15:49.759
about what you're saying, what would you point
to as evidence of this overworking, I guess,

00:15:49.759 --> 00:15:52.649
of the left hemisphere in our society around
us?

00:15:52.649 --> 00:15:53.829
How does it manifest itself?

00:15:53.829 --> 00:15:59.660
Iain: Well, I don't know what it's like in
America, but if you try dealing with any administrative

00:15:59.660 --> 00:16:05.699
body, even with your bank or the telephone
company, you find yourself pushed down an

00:16:05.699 --> 00:16:10.439
algorithm that doesn't have anything to do
with your circumstances.

00:16:10.439 --> 00:16:16.089
And it's over-control, people who are not,
as it were, trained or educated to the point

00:16:16.089 --> 00:16:20.920
where they can think for themselves are dealing
with this as a new situation.

00:16:20.920 --> 00:16:27.560
Instead, they have a repertoire of stale abstractive
situations which they are told to handle in

00:16:27.560 --> 00:16:29.339
certain predicted ways.

00:16:29.339 --> 00:16:34.470
And, unfortunately, life is never like that,
and almost never can be solved in that way.

00:16:34.470 --> 00:16:37.749
I mean, its problems can't be solved in that
way.

00:16:37.749 --> 00:16:42.129
So, this is something that I imagine we all
have experience of.

00:16:42.129 --> 00:16:48.139
Also, our educational system has in my lifetime
gone from being something which encouraged

00:16:48.139 --> 00:16:53.259
free thinking...in fact, the purpose of it
was to encourage people to think differently,

00:16:53.259 --> 00:17:02.139
to question...whatever the received thinking
of the age, also to learn hard a lot of the

00:17:02.139 --> 00:17:05.899
things about the past, which would help you
to interpret the present and give a context

00:17:05.899 --> 00:17:07.439
to it.

00:17:07.439 --> 00:17:11.319
Nowadays people are not interested in, and
don't learn about the past.

00:17:11.319 --> 00:17:15.230
They're over-technically educated, and under-humanistically
educated.

00:17:15.230 --> 00:17:20.209
They don't actually know much about history,
which is, after all, the sum of human experience

00:17:20.209 --> 00:17:21.209
so far.

00:17:21.209 --> 00:17:26.939
They're not encouraged to examine imaginatively
literature which enables you to get into other

00:17:26.939 --> 00:17:29.809
worlds than the one we now live in.

00:17:29.809 --> 00:17:33.279
I mean, the whole point of education is not
to be relevant.

00:17:33.279 --> 00:17:35.649
The worst possible thing is say it must be
relevant.

00:17:35.649 --> 00:17:39.120
What that means is it's got to replicate whatever
it is we're stuck in now.

00:17:39.120 --> 00:17:42.440
But the point of education is to break out
of what we're stuck in now.

00:17:42.440 --> 00:17:45.750
And so, that's the error.

00:17:45.750 --> 00:17:50.820
And, if you now...I mean, I used to teach
literature, and one of the marvelous things

00:17:50.820 --> 00:17:58.110
about it...it was great literatures, absolutely
unique, impossible to rephrase as a bunch

00:17:58.110 --> 00:17:59.110
of ideas.

00:17:59.110 --> 00:18:04.179
It just has a powerful effect on you as an
embodied being, you know.

00:18:04.179 --> 00:18:09.520
And then, now, I mean, I have a daughter who
studied English, and, I mean, what she studied

00:18:09.520 --> 00:18:14.030
was, first of all, very little of what I would
call the canon, which is now considered to

00:18:14.030 --> 00:18:18.919
be somehow irrelevant, but actually without
it you can't understand where we are now.

00:18:18.919 --> 00:18:24.270
If you don't understand history, you don't
understand where you are, like trying to abstract

00:18:24.270 --> 00:18:26.220
yourself from space and say, "Where am I?"

00:18:26.220 --> 00:18:32.630
And you delete all the places around, you
don't know where you are or what you're doing.

00:18:32.630 --> 00:18:38.269
So, that happened, and then, of course, the
attitude is to be superior to the work, because

00:18:38.269 --> 00:18:42.860
you now have information at a point of view
that this poor sucker who wrote this play

00:18:42.860 --> 00:18:44.840
or novel in the past didn't have.

00:18:44.840 --> 00:18:51.480
So, we now tick him off, "Oh, he was a racist,
and a sexist, and a...whatever," and we never

00:18:51.480 --> 00:18:55.679
actually make contact with the thing as a
living thing that is speaking to us imaginatively.

00:18:55.679 --> 00:18:57.400
I mean, this is terrible.

00:18:57.400 --> 00:19:05.570
Brent: So, I mean, I think all of us have
been...all of us have, you know, deep concerns

00:19:05.570 --> 00:19:15.130
about the nature of public discourse today,
you know, the lack of empathy, the lack of

00:19:15.130 --> 00:19:20.770
imagination, and creativity, and problem-solving,
and things like that.

00:19:20.770 --> 00:19:22.700
Do you think that's a function of this phenomenon?

00:19:22.700 --> 00:19:28.570
Iain: Well, I think, with phenomenon, if I'm
right, we are trained to disattend to things

00:19:28.570 --> 00:19:33.179
that are not explicit and that are not in
the foreground, and actually all the interest

00:19:33.179 --> 00:19:36.840
is going on in the background, the context,
the implicit.

00:19:36.840 --> 00:19:41.930
If I'm right, and we're mainly focused on
grabbing the obvious, then we would be living

00:19:41.930 --> 00:19:48.640
in a materialist society that didn't value
spiritual things which simply can't be measured

00:19:48.640 --> 00:19:50.570
and can't be proved.

00:19:50.570 --> 00:19:59.380
It would tend to prefer technical matters
that can be measured to those that are creative.

00:19:59.380 --> 00:20:01.970
It would tend to stultify the insulate.

00:20:01.970 --> 00:20:05.820
And I think that is what is happening, and
people aren't trained to think critically

00:20:05.820 --> 00:20:08.370
about their own opinions, so you get...

00:20:08.370 --> 00:20:12.140
You know, when I was at school I was taught
the purpose of an education is to be able

00:20:12.140 --> 00:20:20.360
to give graded consent or graded descent to
any proposition, and I believe there is nothing

00:20:20.360 --> 00:20:26.940
so good that more and more, and more of it
is just better, and there is nothing so bad

00:20:26.940 --> 00:20:29.460
the little bit of it might not do some good.

00:20:29.460 --> 00:20:34.419
And, if you accept that, and I think it's
hard not to, then the discussion has to be

00:20:34.419 --> 00:20:37.670
about where we should position ourselves on
this continue.

00:20:37.670 --> 00:20:41.549
That is not had as a discussion because people
are polarized.

00:20:41.549 --> 00:20:45.920
The left hemisphere tends to be extremely
crude, and black and white, and sets up oppositions.

00:20:45.920 --> 00:20:49.440
The right hemisphere is better at seeing that
things that look like opposites may cohere.

00:20:49.440 --> 00:20:52.909
They may actually need one another and coexist.

00:20:52.909 --> 00:20:59.970
But I'd like to say that one of the most important
things in which our younger people can see

00:20:59.970 --> 00:21:08.799
the fruits of this way of thinking are all
around us in what we know about the destruction

00:21:08.799 --> 00:21:16.490
of the natural world, the destruction of species,
the poisoning of the seas, the deforestation

00:21:16.490 --> 00:21:26.740
of the earth, in the tendency to grab resources
at the expense of, say, whole indigenous peoples

00:21:26.740 --> 00:21:35.429
that get wiped out, and the over-control of
all lives through monitoring of every kind:

00:21:35.429 --> 00:21:41.590
DNA databases, CCTV cameras, monitoring your
online movements, and so forth.

00:21:41.590 --> 00:21:47.610
And this has become a totalitarian nightmare
already in China, and I worry about this being

00:21:47.610 --> 00:21:48.610
exported here.

00:21:48.610 --> 00:21:50.851
There are already science that it is actually
happening here.

00:21:50.851 --> 00:21:54.750
Brent: It is happening here, but it's interesting
because it's coming through the private sector

00:21:54.750 --> 00:21:56.480
rather than the government.

00:21:56.480 --> 00:21:57.480
Iain: Yeah.

00:21:57.480 --> 00:21:58.480
No.

00:21:58.480 --> 00:21:59.480
That's right.

00:21:59.480 --> 00:22:00.480
Completely, yeah.

00:22:00.480 --> 00:22:01.480
Can I talk about Predictim?

00:22:01.480 --> 00:22:02.480
Brent: Yes, please.

00:22:02.480 --> 00:22:03.480
Go right ahead.

00:22:03.480 --> 00:22:04.480
Iain: Do you know about Predictim?

00:22:04.480 --> 00:22:05.480
Brent: No, please.

00:22:05.480 --> 00:22:06.480
Tell us about it.

00:22:06.480 --> 00:22:07.480
Iain: Okay.

00:22:07.480 --> 00:22:11.220
Well, you know, in China they have this Social
Credit System whereby everything you do is

00:22:11.220 --> 00:22:16.519
monitored and evaluated, and if you talk to
the wrong person, or voice the wrong opinion,

00:22:16.519 --> 00:22:19.769
or buy the wrong thing at a store, this counts
against you.

00:22:19.769 --> 00:22:26.530
And if you get enough black points...bad points,
you can't actually get a job, or take a loan,

00:22:26.530 --> 00:22:30.940
or even move from your town because you can't
buy a train ticket.

00:22:30.940 --> 00:22:35.520
Well, believe it or not, in America there
is a private company called Predictim that

00:22:35.520 --> 00:22:41.530
has developed an algorithm, and these things
always start as for your own protection, you

00:22:41.530 --> 00:22:45.990
know, how everything sinister gets in, to
make things safer.

00:22:45.990 --> 00:22:49.880
And this one is about, how can you get safer
than this babysitting?

00:22:49.880 --> 00:22:54.700
Oh, that really speaks to the core because
everybody wants to protect their child.

00:22:54.700 --> 00:22:58.769
So, there is an algorithm, it will tell you
whether a babysitter is reliable.

00:22:58.769 --> 00:23:00.649
And, how does it do that?

00:23:00.649 --> 00:23:05.910
It monitors your Facebook page, and your Instagram
posts, and everything that they can find out

00:23:05.910 --> 00:23:10.639
about the websites that you visit, and it
looks at your face and your facial expressions

00:23:10.639 --> 00:23:15.870
and movements, and records your opinions,
and it can tell the difference between a funny

00:23:15.870 --> 00:23:20.030
remark, a sarcastic remark, or a mark out
of context ,or anything else, and it creates

00:23:20.030 --> 00:23:24.269
a score, a mindless score that even the people
who created it don't know where it gets the

00:23:24.269 --> 00:23:28.919
data from, and you, the victim of this, don't
know why you're getting a bad score.

00:23:28.919 --> 00:23:34.190
And, that would perhaps not matter very much
if it only happened if you were babysitting,

00:23:34.190 --> 00:23:39.470
but now companies are using Predictim data
to hire people.

00:23:39.470 --> 00:23:45.530
So, if you've got, like you voiced an opinion
that was controversial, you're marked down

00:23:45.530 --> 00:23:47.009
as a troublemaker.

00:23:47.009 --> 00:23:51.580
But the society needs people who are going
to be a little bit rebellious.

00:23:51.580 --> 00:23:53.320
Brent: Right.

00:23:53.320 --> 00:23:54.320
Wow.

00:23:54.320 --> 00:23:55.320
Okay.

00:23:55.320 --> 00:23:59.150
So, let's wrap up on this question.

00:23:59.150 --> 00:24:07.960
If, as you say, this problem of left hemisphere
dominance has occurred in the past, why should

00:24:07.960 --> 00:24:09.850
we worry about it?

00:24:09.850 --> 00:24:14.990
You know, won't it just correct itself over
time the way it has in the past?

00:24:14.990 --> 00:24:19.640
Iain: Well, the last time civilization collapsed
in the West, it took a thousand years for

00:24:19.640 --> 00:24:24.809
one to re-emerge, so that would be concerning,
but not very.

00:24:24.809 --> 00:24:31.279
But, the thing is that in the late Roman Empire,
they were able to commit suicide as a civilization,

00:24:31.279 --> 00:24:36.149
but they weren't able actually physically
to destroy the means of life.

00:24:36.149 --> 00:24:44.340
But, we now have accelerating power, logarithmically
accelerating power, and technology is a wonderful

00:24:44.340 --> 00:24:47.230
thing if you're wise enough to know how to
use it.

00:24:47.230 --> 00:24:52.250
But every technology can be used for good
or ill, and it's only a matter of time before

00:24:52.250 --> 00:24:58.470
that immense power falls into the hands of
people whose desires are not good, not ones

00:24:58.470 --> 00:25:00.629
we would appreciate.

00:25:00.629 --> 00:25:07.460
And so, at the same time that society faces
the possibility of a totalitarian regime,

00:25:07.460 --> 00:25:12.629
quite quickly amassing an enormous amount
of data such that, you know, it just dwarfs

00:25:12.629 --> 00:25:15.830
anything the Soviet system ever thought of,
that can happen.

00:25:15.830 --> 00:25:16.899
It can happen in the West.

00:25:16.899 --> 00:25:19.169
People say it couldn't happen here.

00:25:19.169 --> 00:25:20.169
That's nonsense.

00:25:20.169 --> 00:25:23.299
Democracy is a fragile flower, and it can
easily be trampled on.

00:25:23.299 --> 00:25:28.129
And, you know, we saw totalitarian regimes
arise in places like Germany and Italy in

00:25:28.129 --> 00:25:32.179
the last century, places with a long civilized
history.

00:25:32.179 --> 00:25:33.960
So, that can happen.

00:25:33.960 --> 00:25:39.059
And, the other thing that I'm talking about,
which seems to me the most serious thing of

00:25:39.059 --> 00:25:46.789
all, is that I think there is good evidence
that climate change has now got to the point

00:25:46.789 --> 00:25:53.720
where it is irreversible, and the consequences
of this mean that there will be changes in

00:25:53.720 --> 00:25:56.620
the way in which people can inhabit certain
parts of the world.

00:25:56.620 --> 00:26:00.620
In other words, there'll be mass migrations
of populations away from areas of drought

00:26:00.620 --> 00:26:06.580
and famine, and these will be so large that
they can't be policed or resisted, and there

00:26:06.580 --> 00:26:08.519
will be a breakdown in civil order.

00:26:08.519 --> 00:26:12.720
Now, if that happens, this civilization will
end.

00:26:12.720 --> 00:26:18.220
And it may be not just a matter of a thousand
years, but if the planet is properly despoiled,

00:26:18.220 --> 00:26:20.539
it may be much, much longer.

00:26:20.539 --> 00:26:21.539
The planet will survive.

00:26:21.539 --> 00:26:23.070
People say, "Let's save the planet."

00:26:23.070 --> 00:26:26.330
I would say the planet is amazingly resilient.

00:26:26.330 --> 00:26:28.820
The planet has been through many, many things.

00:26:28.820 --> 00:26:33.450
It's human beings that are fragile, and the
planet will survive, but we won't be there

00:26:33.450 --> 00:26:34.450
to see it.

00:26:34.450 --> 00:26:36.350
And I happen to think that's a great shame.

00:26:36.350 --> 00:26:39.240
Brent: Dr. McGilchrist, it's been a pleasure
having you with us today.

00:26:39.240 --> 00:26:40.669
Iain: Thank you very much.

00:26:40.669 --> 00:26:41.970
Thank you for asking me.

00:26:41.970 --> 00:26:43.669
Brent: Hi, everyone.

00:26:43.669 --> 00:26:46.590
That's the end of our discussion with Iain
McGilchrist.

00:26:46.590 --> 00:26:47.590
Thanks for watching.

00:26:47.590 --> 00:26:52.809
As always, let us know what other topics you'd
like AEI scholars to cover on "Viewpoint."

00:26:52.809 --> 00:26:57.659
And, to learn more about how our brains pay
attention to the world, check out the links

00:26:57.659 --> 00:26:58.950
in the description below.

